===Etymology===
From {{inh|fi|fiu-fin-pro|*otava}}, from {{der|fi|urj-pro|*oča||dam net, enclosure}}. The suffix is unexplained.
Cognates include {{cog|krl|otava}}, {{cog|vot|otava}}, {{cog|lud|adam}}, {{cog|et|odamus}} {{qual|dialectal}}.<ref>{{cite book|last=Kaisa|first=Häkkinen|title=Nykysuomen etymologinen sanakirja|date=2007|publisher=WSOY|isbn=978-951-0-27108-7|edition=4th|language=Finnish}}</ref>

The [[brown bear]] in Finnish actually became known as ''otava'', which is assumed to stem from its resemblance to&mdash;and mythical origin from&mdash;the asterism {{l|fi|Otava||{{l|en|Big Dipper}}}}.<ref>{{cite news|last=Hämäläinen|first=Pirjo|title=Otavassa on orjan merkki|url=http://www.kansanuutiset.fi/mielipiteet/nakokulma/3076910/otavassa-on-orjan-merkki|newspaper=Kansan Uutiset|accessdate=21 April 2014|date=11 November 2013|language=Finnish}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Mykrä |first=Sakari |title=Kahdensadan nimen kontio |url=http://www.suurpedot.fi/www/fi/suurpedot_ja_me/historia/Kahdensadan_nimen_kontio.php |work=Suurpedot.fi |accessdate=21 April 2014 |language=Finnish |deadurl=yes |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130209075432/http://www.suurpedot.fi/www/fi/suurpedot_ja_me/historia/Kahdensadan_nimen_kontio.php |archivedate=9 February 2013 |df= }}</ref> The cartwheel meaning of the word might be related to the Scandinavian interpretation of the Big Dipper as "Charles's Wagon" (Swedish {{m|sv|Karlavagnen}}, Norwegian {{m|no|Karlsvogna}}, or Danish {{m|da|Karlsvognen}}).

===Noun===
{{fi-noun}}

# {{lb|fi|archaic, dialectal}} {{l|en|cartwheel}}
